<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Muscat</h1><p class="st-meta">1507 CE – 1650 CE · capital Muscat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Muscat under Portuguese control, a fortified port guarding the approach to Hormuz until the Omani imams drove the Portuguese out in 1650.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1507 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> attacked the Omani coast and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, then a dependency of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Kingdom of Ormus</a>. Muscat came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> as part of its system of forts protecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> trade and the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For much of the period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> was governed through Hormuzi officials with Portuguese oversight and a garrison. After an Ottoman fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piri-reis">Piri Reis</a> sacked the town in 1552, the Portuguese built the Jalali and Mirani forts in the 1580s, which still overlook the harbour. Muscat grew in importance after the Portuguese lost <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Hormuz</a> in 1622 and moved their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> base there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yaaruba-dynasty">Ya'aruba dynasty</a> took the Omani coast step by step; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-ibn-saif">Sultan ibn Saif</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> in 1650, ending Portuguese rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and beginning Oman's rise as a naval power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-muscat.jpg" alt="Portuguese Muscat" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: Hugo Refachinho · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MASCATE_Ant%C3%B3nio_Bocarro.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1507 CE – 1650 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muscat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Islam (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese Oman, Portuguese Maskat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=portuguese-muscat" aria-label="Portuguese Muscat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6242 1307 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Muscat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6562 1563L4773 1044M6562 1563L6532 1571"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M6532 1571h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6562 1563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6562 1563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Muscat. <a href="/atlas?year=1578&amp;focus=portuguese-muscat">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

